## Running

```sh
npm run dev
```

connects to whatever device `adb` sees. Relevant flags:

- `--serial <id>` pick a device when more than one is attached
- `--replay <file.hrec>` play back a recorded session instead of a live device
- `--record <file.hrec>` mirror a live device and also write the session (video
  and posture samples) to a recording file
- `--probe` run a one-off hardware survey: waits for a device, collects `adb`
  property and `dumpsys` output, checks the scrcpy display list, and samples
  `device_state`/rotation while you fold and rotate the phone. Writes a JSON
  report to the desktop, or to the path given with `--out <file>`
- `--seconds <n>` how long `--probe` samples for (default 90)
- `--user-data <dir>` keep settings in another folder, as the e2e tests do
- `--dry-run-transfers` with `--replay`, run dropped files through the
  transfer queue without a phone, so the progress notices can be tested

A bare file name given to `--record` or `--out` (no directory in the path)
lands on the desktop; a path with a directory is used as given.

Each run writes `hinge.log` to the app's logs directory (truncated at
startup, no rotation) with server output, connection attempts and errors.
An error shown in the window or the probe result page names the log file.

### Folding

The window shows the phone at a constant physical size (the body fills 85
percent of the screen's height) and follows the phone as you fold it:

- Closed or tented, you see the cover screen. Open it and the model unfolds
  to the inner screen; close it and it folds back. The hinge starts moving
  as soon as the phone reports the new posture, and the video moves to the
  other screen once the phone's stream restarts at that screen's size.
- The window keeps its height and grows or shrinks sideways, anchored on
  the right edge (the half with the cameras), so the phone doesn't jump
  around the desktop. It covers both poses while the hinge moves and trims
  to the final size afterwards.
- Half open (Flex mode) with the inner screen on, the model stays flat and
  shows a small "Flex" badge on the top bezel.
- Turn the phone to landscape and the model turns with it around the
  window's centre; touches still land where you click on the video.

### Moving and turning the phone

Straight on, the phone is flat and pixel-accurate. Grab the case to move
or turn it:

- Drag the top or bottom of the case to move the window.
- Drag a side or a corner of the case to turn the phone: sideways spins it
  all the way round, a full 360 degree turntable, and up or down tilts its
  top away or toward you (up to 60 degrees either way). It follows the
  pointer and stays where you leave it; the next drag carries on from
  there.
- Center (the button in the hover menu, or MOD+d) eases it back to
  straight on. So does a double-click on a side or corner.
- The pointer shows what a press does: a hand over the sides and corners,
  a move cursor over the top and bottom. Pressing the screen always touches
  the phone.
- Hold Alt (left or right) to turn the phone from anywhere, the screen
  included; Alt+double-click centres it. Some Linux window managers move
  the window on Alt+drag (Xfce, and KDE depending on its settings), unless
  you change their move modifier.

Turned away from straight on, the phone looks 3D: a perspective lens, a
studio environment with a glass reflection on the screens, the inner
screen's crease catching the light at steep angles, and the phone's real
posture (tented, half open or flat). The look fades in over the first 30
degrees of a turn, so it starts from the flat view without a jump, and
comes back to exactly the flat view on Center.

- While turned, the phone keeps its size and place, and the window grows
  evenly around it for as long as the turn needs the room.
- Touches on the screen still land where you click on the turned video.
- With the display paused (MOD+z) touches stay off, but the case still
  turns the phone, since that never reaches it.
- While turned, the window controls anchor to the window rather than the
  bezel: the menu shows when you hover the window's top edge and sits at its
  top centre, with panels and notices under it.
- Pointer input on the screen is paused while Center eases the phone back;
  keys and shortcuts still work.
- Each phone comes back turned the way you left it.
- In fullscreen the top and bottom don't move anything; turning works as
  usual.

### Windows

From a `cmd.exe` prompt in the installed or unpacked directory:

```bat
hinge.exe --serial 10.0.0.23:5555
hinge.exe --probe --seconds 60
```

The window is frameless. Drag the phone's top or bottom edge to move it,
move the pointer to its top edge for its controls, quit with MOD+q, or close it with Alt+F4 or from the taskbar.
Starting hinge again while it runs brings the open window to the front.

### Window controls

Hovering the bezel along the phone's top edge shows a small bar of buttons
(the screen below it stays the phone's, so pulling down the notification
shade from the top still works):

- **Devices** lists the phones adb can see (USB, Wi-Fi and mDNS), lets you
  switch to another one, switch a USB phone to Wi-Fi, disconnect a Wi-Fi
  phone, or pair a new one.
- **Center** turns the phone back to straight on (MOD+d).
- **Colour** picks the model's colourway: Jet Black, Blue Shadow, Silver
  Shadow or Mint.
- **Pin** keeps the window above other windows.
- **Settings** has the shortcut modifier, notification mirroring, phone
  audio, the video codec, bit rate, size and frame rate limits, and how
  smoothly and how far the mouse wheel scrolls the phone. Stream settings
  apply the next time the phone connects.
- **Close** quits.

Panels open inside the window (it grows while one needs the room) and close
with Escape or a click elsewhere. Short notices appear under the phone's top
bezel for three seconds; the Devices panel also shows pairing and connection
errors in its own status line.

### Shortcuts

MOD is left Alt by default on Windows, which keeps most Win+letter
combinations for itself, and left Alt or left Super on Linux. It can be any
mix of left or right Alt, Super and Ctrl in Settings. The table follows
[scrcpy's](https://github.com/Genymobile/scrcpy/blob/master/doc/shortcuts.md).

| Action | Shortcut |
| --- | --- |
| Quit | MOD+q |
| Fullscreen | MOD+f or F11 |
| Screen at 1:1 (one video pixel per display pixel) | MOD+g |
| Shrink the window back after a panel | MOD+w |
| Bigger or smaller, 10 percent steps | MOD+wheel |
| Turn the phone back to straight on | MOD+d |
| Turn the phone model from anywhere, or centre it | Alt+drag, Alt+double-click |
| Pause display, or resume | MOD+z, MOD+Shift+z |
| Reset video capture | MOD+Shift+r |
| HOME | MOD+h or middle click |
| BACK | MOD+b, MOD+Backspace or right click |
| APP_SWITCH | MOD+s or 4th mouse button |
| MENU | MOD+m |
| Volume up, down | MOD+Up, MOD+Down |
| POWER | MOD+p |
| Phone screen off, keep mirroring | MOD+o |
| Phone screen on | MOD+Shift+o |
| Rotate the phone's screen | MOD+r |
| Notification panel | MOD+n or 5th mouse button |
| Quick settings panel | MOD+n pressed twice |
| Collapse the panels | MOD+Shift+n |
| Copy, cut on the phone | MOD+c, MOD+x |
| Paste the PC clipboard | Ctrl+v or MOD+v |
| Type the PC clipboard as keystrokes | MOD+Shift+v |
| Frame rate on the bottom bezel | MOD+i |
| Pinch to zoom or rotate | Ctrl+drag |
| Two-finger vertical slide | Shift+drag |
| Two-finger horizontal slide | Ctrl+Shift+drag |

Other Ctrl shortcuts go to the phone, unless Ctrl is set as MOD. AltGr
(Ctrl+Alt on Windows) types its character. The clipboard syncs both ways on its own: copy on the
phone and paste on the PC.

### Phone audio

Phones on Android 11 or newer play their audio through the PC. If the phone
can't share audio, hinge says so once and carries on without it; if the
audio stops partway while the video goes on, a notice says that too. Turn
it off in Settings.

### Notifications

While the phone is streaming, its notifications appear on the PC too:
ongoing, foreground-service, group-summary and below-default-importance
ones are left out, and nothing pops while the hinge window is focused.
Clicking one brings hinge to the front and opens the app on the phone.
Turn it off in Settings.

### Files

Drop files on the window. An `.apk` is installed, replacing an older version
of the same app; anything else is copied to the phone's `Download` folder
under its own name. Files go one at a time, each with a progress notice.

### Wi-Fi

To go wireless with the phone plugged in, open Devices and press Wi-Fi next
to it; hinge restarts adb on the phone in TCP mode and connects to its Wi-Fi
address. Unplug the cable once the new entry shows as current.

To pair without a cable, on the phone open Developer options, Wireless
debugging, Pair device with pairing code. Enter the IP address and port and
the six-digit code shown there under Pair new device. If the phone isn't
found on the network afterwards, enter the port shown on the Wireless
debugging screen itself (it differs from the pairing port) and press
Connect.

A Wi-Fi phone that drops off (Wi-Fi toggled, phone asleep) is reconnected
with a growing delay, up to 15 seconds between tries, until it comes back
or you pick another device. The last phone used connects on its own at the
next launch, over whichever connection it shows up on: hinge knows a phone
by its serial number, so its USB, Wi-Fi and mDNS entries and a wireless
port that changed since last time all count as the same phone.
Disconnect drops the phone's Wi-Fi connection and keeps hinge from picking
it up again until you choose it in Devices.

### Settings file

Settings are saved as `settings.json` in the app's data folder:
`%APPDATA%\hinge\settings.json` on Windows and
`~/.config/hinge/settings.json` on Linux. The Settings panel shows the full
path. A file that can't be read is kept as `settings.json.bad` and defaults
are used, and a single setting with a wrong value falls back to its
default on its own. Besides the panel's options it holds the colourway,
the last phone, and for each phone (by serial number) the window's
position, the size you picked and how the model was turned. The last
phone's position, size and turn are applied at startup, before it connects.

## Development commands

```sh
npm run dev          # electron-vite dev server
npm run build         # production build
npm run typecheck     # tsc -b
npm test              # vitest
npm run test:e2e       # playwright, replays a synthetic fold recording end to end
npm run pack:win       # unpacked Windows build in dist/win-unpacked
npm run pack:linux     # unpacked Linux build in dist/linux-unpacked
npm run pack:release   # installer, portable exe, AppImage and deb in dist/
```

`pack:release` builds the Windows files on Linux too, through wine, which
electron-builder needs to write the icon, product name and version into
`hinge.exe` and to run the 32-bit NSIS installer stub:

```sh
sudo dpkg --add-architecture i386 && sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install --no-install-recommends wine wine64 wine32:i386
```

### Tests and releases

There is no CI. Unit tests and the typecheck run in the `pre-push` hook
(`pre-commit install --hook-type pre-commit --hook-type pre-push`), and the
GPU e2e suite runs on demand through `scripts/e2e-remote.sh` (below).

Releases are cut locally with the release-please CLI, which reads the
Conventional Commit history, then the artefacts from `npm run pack:release`
are attached to the GitHub release:

```sh
npx release-please release-pr --repo-url=Garulf/hinge --token=$(gh auth token)
# merge the release PR, then
npx release-please github-release --repo-url=Garulf/hinge --token=$(gh auth token)
npm run pack:release
gh release upload v<version> dist/*-setup.exe dist/*-portable.exe dist/*.AppImage dist/*.deb
```

### Remote testing

The dev container has no GPU, so `npm run test:e2e` here runs under Xvfb with
SwiftShader software GL, which is slow and skips the app's lighting and
reflections. `HINGE_REMOTE=<ssh alias> scripts/e2e-remote.sh [playwright
args]` rsyncs the working tree to a host with a real GPU, installs
dependencies there only when `package-lock.json` changed, builds, runs the
suite against the real display with `HINGE_E2E_REAL_GPU=1` set (the test
skips its SwiftShader flags when that's set), and copies `test-results/`
back under `test-results/remote-<host>/`. On a macOS host the test step runs
as a self-owned LaunchAgent bootstrapped into the logged-in user's GUI
session rather than being spawned straight from the ssh shell, since a
plain ssh session can't reach the WindowServer and fixing that with
`launchctl asuser` needs root.

Set `HINGE_REAL_RECORDING=<file.hrec>` to also replay a real `--record`
capture; that test passes once it has seen the model settle on the inner
screen after the cover and back again. `HINGE_REAL_RECORDING_TIMEOUT_MS`
(default 180000) bounds how long it waits.

`HINGE_ADB_REMOTE=<ssh alias> scripts/adb-remote.sh start|stop|status`
forwards a local port to another host's adb server over ssh, for a phone
that's only reachable from there (wireless debugging paired to a different
machine). `start` opens a background tunnel from `127.0.0.1:15037` here to
`127.0.0.1:5037` on the host, `status` runs `adb -H 127.0.0.1 -P 15037
devices` through it, and `stop` closes it; it never starts, stops, or
restarts an adb server anywhere.
